#: src/references/slices.md:6
msgid "Slices"
msgstr ""

#: src/references/slices.md:8
msgid "A slice gives you a view into a larger collection:"
msgstr ""

#: src/references/slices.md:25
msgid "Slices borrow data from the sliced type."
msgstr ""

#: src/references/slices.md:30
msgid ""
"We create a slice by borrowing `a` and specifying the starting and ending "
"indexes in brackets."
msgstr ""

#: src/references/slices.md:33
msgid ""
"If the slice starts at index 0, Rust’s range syntax allows us to drop the "
"starting index, meaning that `&a[0..a.len()]` and `&a[..a.len()]` are "
"identical."
msgstr ""

#: src/references/slices.md:37
msgid ""
"The same is true for the last index, so `&a[2..a.len()]` and `&a[2..]` are "
"identical."
msgstr ""

#: src/references/slices.md:40
msgid ""
"To easily create a slice of the full array, we can therefore use `&a[..]`."
msgstr ""

#: src/references/slices.md:42
msgid ""
"`s` is a reference to a slice of `i32`s. Notice that the type of `s` "
"(`&[i32]`) no longer mentions the array length. This allows us to perform "
"computation on slices of different sizes."
msgstr ""

#: src/references/slices.md:46
msgid ""
"Slices always borrow from another object. In this example, `a` has to remain "
"'alive' (in scope) for at least as long as our slice."
msgstr ""

#: src/references/slices.md:49
msgid "You can't \"grow\" a slice once it's created:"
msgstr ""

#: src/references/slices.md:50
msgid ""
"You can't append elements of the slice, since it doesn't own the backing "
"buffer."
msgstr ""

#: src/references/slices.md:52
msgid ""
"You can't grow a slice to point to a larger section of the backing buffer. A "
"slice does not have information about the length of the underlying buffer "
"and so you can't know how large the slice can be grown."
msgstr ""

#: src/references/slices.md:55
msgid ""
"To get a larger slice you have to go back to the original buffer and create "
"a larger slice from there."
msgstr ""
